WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.680
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.640
There is undeniable evidence

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.120
that regular physical activity will aid
in the prevention of chronic disease.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.440
But how much
exercise is enough?

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.480
Here’s our first story.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.280
Compared
to sedentary individuals,

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.080
physically active men
and women may have

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.880
an approximately 30 percent
lower risk of premature death.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.960
Even just walking 15 minutes
a day may reduce mortality risk

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.040
by 15 percent.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.120
Mortality rates continue
to drop about four percent

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.480
for each additional 15 minutes
of moderate exercise a day

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.640
like walking, plateauing out
at about 90 minutes a day.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.800
What does that translate to
in terms of time?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.720
The life expectancy for those
walking just 15 minutes a day

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.200
would be
about three years longer

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.480
compared to those
who don't exercise regularly,

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.160
and those meeting
the recommended 30 minutes

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.280
a day live
about four years longer.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.440
If you jog a few hours a week
or are in the top five percent

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.400
of cardiorespiratory fitness,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.120
you may live five
or six years longer.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.520
Exercising for 20 minutes
may add an hour to your life.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.680
So, for all those who say
they don't have time

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.840
to work out,
exercising gives us

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.040
like a three-to-one
return on investment.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.480
Give 20 minutes of your life
to get 60 minutes of life.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.880
Beyond that, there's a bit
of diminishing returns,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.160
but exercise an hour a day
and maybe get back more time

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.040
than you put in.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.160
The return for vigorous exercise
may be even greater,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.760
as much as seven to one.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.760
An hour of running
could potentially extend

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.160
your life seven hours.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.600
Running even 5 to 10 minutes
a day at a jogging pace

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.200
(less than six miles
(9.70 km) per hour)

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.400
may net the same
longevity benefits

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.280
of the 15 minutes
of daily walking.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.520
Similarly, running fifteen
to twenty minutes a day may be

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.360
equivalent to walking an hour
a day, and twenty-five minutes

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.600
of running can match walking's
maximum mortality benefits

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.600
that top off
at around 100 minutes.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.760
The benefits of running,
however, continue to increase

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.480
until about 45 minutes a day.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.240
Walking is the most
commonly reported exercise,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.600
and though it is not
as time-effective

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.200
as running, it's easier
and safer to start and sustain,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.200
particularly if you,
like much of the population,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.760
are starting out sedentary.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.960
Walking is among the safest
of physical activities,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.760
averaging only about one injury
per thousand hours.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.880
Not using your cell phone
and walking against traffic

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.320
when on roads without sidewalks
can reduce pedestrian injuries.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.800
In contrast, nearly 70 percent
of serious runners

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.400
become injured over
a year-long period,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.400
suffering most commonly
from knee problems.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.400
There does not appear
to be an increased risk

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.240
of knee osteoarthritis, though,
in all but the most elite

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.040
long-distance runners who have
about three times higher odds.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.240
Recreational runners
do not appear

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.600
to be at higher risk.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.040
The current official
physical activity guidelines

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.000
recommend adults get
at least 150 minutes a week

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.120
of moderate aerobic exercise,
which comes out to be

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.560
a little more
than 20 minutes a day.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.440
That's actually down
from previous recommendations

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.440
from the Surgeon General,
the CDC,

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.280
and the American College
of Sports Medicine

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.960
for at least 30 minutes a day.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.240
This is construed as
a "trade-off between optimizing

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.520
health outcomes and minimizing
requirements for individuals."

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.840
The exercise authorities
seem to have fallen

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.160
into the same trap
as the nutrition authorities,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.520
recommending what they think
may be achievable,

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.000
rather than simply informing you
what the science says

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.360
and letting you
make up your own mind.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.960
They already emphasize
that any physical activity

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.640
is better than none,
so why not stop patronizing

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.000
the public and just tell
everyone the truth?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.240
The optimal "dose" of exercise
for maximizing longevity

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.920
remains uncertain,
but a pooled analysis

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.400
of more than a half million men
and women followed

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.560
for an average
of more than a dozen years

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.360
found that mortality rates
bottomed out at about 90 minutes

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.240
a day of moderate intensity
physical activity

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.000
(mostly walking).

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.120
Compared
to no regular exercise, though,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.360
exercising
just about 30 minutes a day

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.360
gets you about 80 percent
of the way

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.440
to that maximum
exercise benefit.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.200
Older adults are also encouraged
to do both muscle strengthening

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.040
and flexibility training
at least twice a week,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.640
along with incorporating
balance exercises.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.920
Instead of exercise measured
in minutes,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.800
what about measured in steps?

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.480
Nonagenarians—those in their
90s—in the Sardinian blue zone

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.840
average about 12,000 steps
a day just living their lives.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.280
Based on a representative sample
of thousands of U.S. adults,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.000
those getting 12,000 steps
a day had a 65 percent

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.840
lower risk of dying over
the subsequent decade compared

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.360
to those getting only
4,000 steps a day.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.320
Even just getting
8,000 steps a day

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.360
appeared to cut the risk
of premature death in half.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.120
A study of more
than 15,000 older U.S. women

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.480
found that just 4,400 steps
a day could reduce

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.880
mortality rates compared
to 2,700 steps a day,

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.360
raising the question:
"Is 4,400 steps per day

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.640
the new 10,000 steps per day?"

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:56.520
Where did the 10,000 steps a day
recommendation even come from?

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.080
Surprisingly,
it likely originated

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.240
as the brand-name
of a Japanese pedometer

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.520
sold in 1965 called Manpo-kei
or "10,000 steps meter."

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.360
In terms of walking,
the recommendation

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.280
for 150 minutes a week
of moderate intensity

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.640
aerobic exercise
gets us to approximately

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.640
7,000 steps a day.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.920
The maximum longevity dose
of around 90 minutes a day

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.960
(technically 22.5 to 40
so-called MET-hours a week)

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.960
would give most people a total
of about 13,500 steps per day.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:33.720
In our next story,

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.840
we discover how the evidence
supporting the overall health benefits

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.440
of physical activity is overwhelming.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.040
Researchers who accept grants
from the Coca-Cola Company call

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.960
physical inactivity "the
greatest public health problem

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.240
of the 21st century."

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.520
Actually, physical inactivity
ranks down at number 10

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.680
for risk factors for death
in the United States

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.560
and number 11 for risk factors
for disability.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.720
And globally, in terms of years
of healthy life lost,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.000
inactivity doesn't even break
into the top 20.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.120
As we've learned,
diet is our greatest killer,

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.320
followed by smoking.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.040
A media analysis found hundreds
of news articles claiming

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.920
that prolonged daily sitting
is as bad or even worse

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.720
than smoking.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.920
This is decidedly not the case.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.440
Smoking is expected to cause
a billion deaths this century.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:30.240
Tobacco is responsible for up
to more than 10 times greater

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.160
mortality risk,
comparing the heaviest smokers

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.200
versus the heaviest sitters.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:39.720
What role does physical activity
play in longevity?

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.440
In terms of combatting
the hallmarks of aging,

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:46.280
aerobic exercise can induce
autophagy, lower inflammation,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.600
decrease DNA damage,
and facilitate DNA repair.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.240
A meta-analysis of cohort
studies of middle-aged

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.680
and older individuals with
follow-ups as long as 20 years

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.080
found that exercising adults
were more likely

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.800
to age successfully
than sedentary individuals.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.960
Population studies
have found a correlation

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.840
between regular aerobic exercise
and decreased risk

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.680
of at least 35
different diseases.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.840
But what have interventional
trials proven in terms

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.520
of cause-and-effect?

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.440
Randomized controlled trials
of older adults

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.960
have demonstrated
that physical activity

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.120
can improve muscle mass,
strength, balance,

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.920
and mobility,
decrease the risk of falls

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.000
and potential fractures,
while helping

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.560
to minimize bone loss.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.160
Exercise may also
improve cognition, enhance mood,

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:39.640
successfully treat depression
(as well as the prescription of

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.760
the antidepressant drug Zoloft),
improve erectile function

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.080
in men, and generally improve
quality of life.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.440
The evidence supporting
the overall health benefits

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:54.080
of physical activity
are overwhelming.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.560
Exercise is medicine.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.720
Artery stiffness is recognized
as a barometer of aging,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.720
supporting
the 17th century axiom from

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.920
the English Hippocrates that
"man is as old as his arteries."

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.120
Normally, large arteries
in the body expand

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.320
and recoil with every heartbeat.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.040
(That's the pulse you feel
in your wrist.)

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.920
This helps cushion the pressure
waves to prevent damage

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.200
to tiny sensitive blood vessels
in organs like your brain

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:24.680
and kidneys.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.160
Thankfully,
not all age-related stiffening

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:28.520
is inevitable.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.960
Endurance training men
and women have artery elasticity

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.880
closer to that of younger
sedentary individuals,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.160
a status that can be achieved
after daily brisk walks

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.080
or jogging for
just three months.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.400
This may help explain
why those who exercise

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.120
appear to live so much longer.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.880
A single exercise session
can improve insulin sensitivity

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:51.880
for up to 17 hours.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.480
Exercise is such powerful
medicine that researchers

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:55.960
at Stanford
and the London School

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:59.440
found that exercise
may work as well as drugs

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.120
for patients
with coronary heart disease,

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.160
heart failure, and pre-diabetes,
and even better

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.560
than some medications
for stroke.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.880
They suggested that
drug companies should perhaps

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:13.240
be required to compare
any new chronic disease drugs

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.800
head-to-head against exercise,
as patients deserve

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.240
to understand
the relative impact

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.120
that physical activity
might have on their condition.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.800
Exercise has been described as
the "only intervention that has

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.880
shown a remarkable efficacy
for...increasing mean

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.988
and maximum lifespan
in humans."

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.120
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.920
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.800
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.560
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:47.320
If you see any graphs, charts,
graphics, images, or studies mentioned here,

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:50.760
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.060
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:57.680
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

